// D-FAIL-CONV2=A (ratified 2026-08-17, card #2018): the standard library declares
// the conversion for its own error family. A program can write `fn run() !` and
// pass a Core failure up with `?` without declaring anything, because these
// declarations already sit on the existing D-ERR-CONV rail. One source; every
// tier lowers the same `impl <CoreError> -> Err` block, the same way
// `Prelude/Units.jet` ships one dimension catalog.
//
// Sema injects only the conversions a module actually exercises — the `?`
// operators whose error type is a family member converting into the default
// `Err`. Unused members are not copied into the module, so a defect in one
// conversion cannot break a program that never produces that error.
//
// An author who wants the failure named still writes the named form
// (`fn run() !JSONError`), and that stays correct: this file only makes the
// unnamed form work.
//
// D-FAIL-CONV1=A keeps the rest of the rule intact. A user error type still
// needs its own `impl MyError -> Err { … }`; nothing here weakens that. A module
// that declares its own conversion for one of these pairs wins over the shipped
// one, so a package can still say how its program reports a Core failure.
//
// Family membership is derived, not chosen by taste. A Core error type belongs
// here when all three hold:
//   1. the Prelude registers it printable — `auto_printable` in
//      `TraitRegistry::register_synthetic_display_debug`, so the Prelude already
//      owns its failure text;
//   2. a Core signature returns it in the error position of a `T !E` result, so
//      a program can actually receive one; and
//   3. showing it is not prohibited.
//
// Deliberately outside the family:
//   * `CryptoError` — a secret-bearing crypto type must never flatten into a
//     user-visible message (E0915). Naming it stays explicit.
//   * `TaskFailure` — D-FAIL-CARRIER1 gives cancellation and deadline their own
//     carrier and report; it is not a library error family member.
//   * `[FieldError]` — the Core decode surface returns a list, and a conversion
//     source is one named type.
//   * component types (`DataErrorKind`, `EncodingCause`, `IOContext`,
//     `NetErrorDetail`, `NetDnsError`, …) — no Core call fails with one.

impl BuildError -> Err {
    return Err("{self}")
}

impl BrowserError -> Err {
    return Err("{self}")
}

impl CBORError -> Err {
    return Err("{self}")
}

impl DBError -> Err {
    return Err("{self}")
}

impl DataError -> Err {
    return Err("{self}")
}

impl EncodingError -> Err {
    return Err("{self}")
}

impl EnvError -> Err {
    return Err("{self}")
}

impl HTTPError -> Err {
    return Err("{self}")
}

impl IOError -> Err {
    return Err("{self}")
}

impl JSONError -> Err {
    return Err("{self}")
}

impl NetError -> Err {
    return Err("{self}")
}

impl RangeError -> Err {
    return Err("{self}")
}

impl TextError -> Err {
    return Err("{self}")
}

impl UTF8Error -> Err {
    return Err("{self}")
}

impl WsError -> Err {
    return Err("{self}")
}

impl XMLError -> Err {
    return Err("{self}")
}
